const BIOLOGY: Row[] = [
  ["cells", 0, "Cells", "The unit of life: animal, plant, and specialised.", "All life is cells. Animal cells: nucleus (DNA), cytoplasm, membrane, mitochondria. Plant cells add chloroplasts, a cellulose wall and a vacuole. Specialisation: red cells lose their nucleus to carry more oxygen; sperm pack mitochondria; root hair cells grow huge surface area. Structure always serves function.", [], []],
  ["microscopy", 1, "Microscopy", "Seeing the very small: optical to electron.", "Optical microscopes magnify ~1500× — enough for cells and nuclei. Electron microscopes reach individual organelles and viruses (millions of ×). Magnification = image ÷ real size; standard form handles the scales: a 30 μm cell is 3 × 10⁻⁵ m. Resolution, not just magnification, separates close points.", ["cells", "standard-form"], []],
  ["enzymes", 1, "Enzymes", "Biological catalysts: specific, temperature- and pH-sensitive.", "Enzymes are protein catalysts with active sites shaped for one substrate — lock and key. Denaturation: too hot or wrong pH warps the site and activity dies. Rate climbs with temperature until proteins begin to fail (~37 °C for humans). Substrate concentration saturates: every enzyme busy is maximum rate.", ["cells", "proportion"], []],
  ["digestion", 1, "Digestion", "Breaking food into absorbable molecules.", "Mechanical breakdown (teeth) plus enzymes: amylase → starch to sugar, protease → protein to amino acids, lipase → fat to fatty acids + glycerol. Bile emulsifies fat and neutralises stomach acid. Villi multiply the small intestine's surface — absorption is a surface-area game.", ["enzymes"], []],
  ["circulation", 2, "Circulatory system", "Double circulation: heart, lungs, body.", "Right heart pumps to lungs (oxygen in, CO₂ out); left heart pumps to body — twice through the heart per circuit. Arteries take blood away (thick, high pressure), veins return (valves), capillaries exchange (one cell thick). The pacemaker sets ~70 bpm; exercise multiplies output. Blockage is the world's leading cause of death.", ["cells", "pressure-fluids"], []],
  ["breathing-gas", 1, "Gas exchange", "Lungs, alveoli, and diffusion of O₂ and CO₂.", "Breathing ventilates alveoli — 300 million air sacs, folded like a tennis court's surface, one cell thick, moist. O₂ diffuses into blood, CO₂ out, down concentration gradients. Intercostal muscles and the diaphragm change chest volume: breathe in, volume up, pressure down, air flows in.", ["cells", "diffusion"], []],
  ["diffusion", 1, "Diffusion, osmosis & transport", "Particles moving down gradients.", "Diffusion: particles spread from high to low concentration — smell crossing a room, oxygen into blood. Osmosis is water's version through a partially permeable membrane. Active transport pumps against the gradient using energy — root cells mining minerals. Surface area : volume ratio rules it all: small is fast, big needs transport systems.", ["cells"], []],
  ["photosynthesis", 1, "Photosynthesis", "Light, CO₂ and water into glucose.", "6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ in chloroplasts. Limiting factors: light intensity, CO₂ concentration, temperature — the graph plateaus as each saturates. Glucose fuels growth, respiration, and storage as starch. Every food chain starts here: plants bank sunlight as chemical energy.", ["enzymes"], []],
  ["respiration", 1, "Respiration", "Releasing energy from glucose.", "Aerobic: glucose + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O + lots of energy (mitochondria). Anaerobic (no oxygen): glucose → lactic acid (muscle ache, oxygen debt) or → ethanol + CO₂ (yeast, fermentation). Exercise raises rate; recovery repays the debt. Respiration is not breathing — it is the chemistry inside every cell.", ["enzymes", "energy-conservation"], []],
  ["nervous-system", 2, "Nervous system", "Fast electrical signalling: reflexes and reactions.", "Receptors detect, neurons carry, effectors respond. Reflex arc: sensory neuron → relay neuron → motor neuron — automatic, fast, no brain needed (hot pan, hand moves). Synapses pass signals chemically; myelin insulates for speed. Reaction time measures the circuit's health.", ["cells"], []],
  ["hormones", 2, "Hormones & homeostasis", "Chemical messengers keeping the inside constant.", "Endocrine glands release hormones into blood — slower than nerves, longer-lasting. Homeostasis holds constants: blood glucose (insulin lowers, glucagon raises), body temperature 37 °C (sweat, shiver, vasodilation), water balance (ADH, kidneys). Negative feedback: the deviation itself triggers the correction.", ["nervous-system", "diffusion"], []],
  ["genetics", 2, "Genetics & inheritance", "DNA, genes, and how traits pass on.", "DNA is the double helix; genes are instructions for proteins. Alleles are gene versions: dominant (capital) shows if present, recessive needs both copies. Punnett squares predict offspring ratios: two carriers Aa × Aa → 3 : 1. Sex determination: XX female, XY male. Meiosis halves chromosomes so children get one set from each parent.", ["cells", "probability-basics"], []],
  ["evolution", 3, "Evolution & selection", "Variation, selection, and change over generations.", "Mutations create variation; selection keeps the fittest — those who reproduce more pass on their alleles. Darwin's finches: beaks match food sources. Antibiotic resistance is evolution in a petri dish: survive the drug, multiply, repeat. Speciation follows isolation — separated populations drift apart until they can no longer interbreed.", ["genetics"], []],
  ["ecosystems", 2, "Ecosystems & cycles", "Food webs, energy loss, and material cycles.", "Producers capture sunlight; consumers eat; decomposers recycle. Energy transfers lose ~90% per level — hence short food chains and few top predators. Carbon cycle: photosynthesis in, respiration and combustion out. Water cycle: evaporation, condensation, precipitation. Nitrogen cycle: bacteria make the air fertile.", ["photosynthesis", "respiration"], []],
  ["biodiversity", 3, "Biodiversity & human impact", "Variety of life, and what we are doing to it.", "Biodiversity — species, genetic and ecosystem variety — makes systems resilient. Human pressures: habitat loss, pollution, overfishing, invasive species, climate change. Conservation: reserves, seed banks, captive breeding, sustainable quotas. The tension: development now vs stability later — and the maths of tipping points.", ["ecosystems"], []],
  ["immune-health", 3, "Health, disease & immunity", "Pathogens, defence, and vaccines.", "Pathogens: viruses (hijack cells), bacteria (toxins), fungi, protists. Barriers first (skin, mucus, acid), then white cells: phagocytosis, then antibodies shaped to one antigen. Vaccines train the system safely — memory cells stand ready. Antivirals are hard; antibiotics only work on bacteria — misuse breeds resistance.", ["cells", "evolution"], []],
];

